---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/adgm-divorce-abu-dhabi/"
  {hreflang}
  lang="en"
  lastmod="2026-09-21"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-4">
    <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
  </div>

  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-tag">Abu Dhabi Divorce Guide · ADGM Courts 2026</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="hero-desc">
        Many expats search for an "ADGM divorce" hoping for an English-language, common-law process in Abu Dhabi. The ADGM Courts cannot provide one. They are civil and commercial courts, and family cases sit outside their remit. The good news is that Abu Dhabi already has what most people are looking for: a Civil Family Court for non-Muslims that works in Arabic and English and grants divorce without anyone having to prove fault.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated September 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-layout">
    <main class="main-content">

      <div class="answer-box">
        <h2>The short answer</h2>
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li><strong>The ADGM Courts cannot grant a divorce.</strong> They hear civil and commercial cases and handle the ADGM non-Muslim wills registry and probate.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-Muslims divorce at the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court,</strong> created under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021. It works in Arabic and English and grants no-fault divorce.</li>
          <li><strong>ADGM companies and assets</strong> are dealt with by the family court as part of the financial claims, not by the ADGM Courts.</li>
          <li><strong>ADGM wills</strong> are worth reviewing after a divorce.</li>
          <li><strong>The DIFC Courts in Dubai</strong> cannot grant a divorce either.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What the ADGM Courts Actually Do</h2>
        <p>
          Abu Dhabi Global Market (ADGM) is Abu Dhabi's international financial centre on Al Maryah Island. Like the DIFC in Dubai, it has its own legal system built on English common law and its own English-language courts, the ADGM Courts, with a Court of First Instance and a Court of Appeal. The set-up is real and well regarded, and it is the reason the idea of an "ADGM divorce" keeps coming up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ADGM Courts exist to resolve civil and commercial disputes: contracts, banking and finance, companies and shareholders, employment within ADGM, and other claims connected to the financial centre. Their one role that touches family life is succession. ADGM runs a wills registry for non-Muslims, and its courts handle probate of the wills registered there.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the ADGM Courts do not do is hear family cases. They cannot grant a divorce, and they do not decide custody, alimony, child maintenance or financial claims between spouses. If a website suggests you can obtain an ADGM divorce decree, treat it as marketing shorthand rather than a description of the court's powers.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>In one line</h3>
          <p>ADGM is where many people keep their wills and their companies, not where they end their marriages. For the divorce itself, Abu Dhabi has a separate court built for exactly that job.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Why People Search for an ADGM Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          The search is usually shorthand for a wish list: proceedings in English, a court that reasons in a way that feels familiar from home, and a process that does not require one spouse to prove the other did something wrong. ADGM's reputation ticks all three boxes, so it becomes the search term.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some of the confusion comes from online content that describes ADGM as a "third track" for divorce, quotes ADGM filing fees for a divorce petition, or promises English-style needs and sharing orders. None of that reflects how the courts work. There is no ADGM divorce petition, no ADGM family fee scale and no ADGM financial remedy on divorce.
        </p>
        <p>
          The instinct behind the search is sound, though. Every item on that wish list is available in Abu Dhabi through the correct court: English-language proceedings, a civil rather than religious framework, and no-fault divorce are all features of the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court. The same question comes up in Dubai, and our guide to <a href="/en/difc-divorce-dubai/">DIFC divorce in Dubai</a> reaches the same conclusion about the DIFC Courts.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Where Non-Muslims Really Divorce in Abu Dhabi</h2>
        <p>
          The venue is the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, part of the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department (ADJD) and also known as the Non-Muslim Family Court. It was created under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021, which gave non-Muslims in the emirate a civil personal status regime before the federal civil law followed in 2022, and it has handled civil marriages, divorces and related claims since it opened at the end of 2021. The ADJD describes the court and its services on its <a href="https://www.adjd.gov.ae/EN/Pages/nonmuslimsfamilycourt.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank">official Non-Muslim Family Court page</a>.
        </p>
        <h3>Why it is the court most people are really looking for</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Arabic and English:</strong> the court works bilingually, so hearings and documents are accessible to expats who do not read Arabic.</li>
          <li><strong>No-fault divorce:</strong> either spouse can ask for a divorce without proving harm, misconduct or any particular ground.</li>
          <li><strong>Speed:</strong> there is no mandatory reconciliation stage, and an uncontested case often needs only a single hearing.</li>
          <li><strong>Civil rules on the family issues:</strong> custody, alimony and other financial claims are decided under the civil regime, with joint custody as the starting point.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Muslim couples in Abu Dhabi use the Abu Dhabi Personal Status Court instead, under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024. If you live in Dubai or one of the northern emirates, the civil route for non-Muslims runs through the local personal status court under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022. Our guide to <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce in the UAE</a> explains how that works, and the <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai vs Abu Dhabi divorce comparison</a> sets the two emirates side by side.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How a Civil Family Court Divorce Works, Step by Step</h2>
        <p>
          The outline below is deliberately high level. Our full walkthrough of <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a> covers the forms, fees and timelines in detail.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">1</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Confirm you can use the civil route</h3>
              <p>The court is designed for non-Muslims with a connection to Abu Dhabi, such as residence in the emirate or a civil marriage registered there. A short jurisdiction check with a lawyer avoids a filing that gets turned away.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">2</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Gather your documents</h3>
              <p>Typically the marriage certificate, passports and Emirates IDs for both spouses, and any marriage or settlement agreement. Foreign documents may need translation and attestation. Our <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents checklist</a> lists what to prepare.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">3</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Submit the application</h3>
              <p>Applications go in through the ADJD channels, such as an approved typing centre or the ADJD smart services. A lawyer can file for you under a power of attorney.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">4</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Attend the hearing</h3>
              <p>Either spouse can ask for the divorce without giving reasons. In an uncontested case the divorce is often granted at a single hearing, and there is no mandatory reconciliation stage first.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">5</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Agree or apply for the family orders</h3>
              <p>Custody, alimony, child support and other financial claims are either agreed between you or decided by the court under the civil rules. Our guides to <a href="/en/child-custody-abu-dhabi/">child custody in Abu Dhabi</a> and <a href="/en/alimony-abu-dhabi/">alimony in Abu Dhabi</a> explain what to expect.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num">6</div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <h3>Collect and attest the judgment</h3>
              <p>Once the judgment is issued, it can be attested for use abroad, for example to update your civil status at home or to remarry.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Assets or Companies Held in ADGM</h2>
        <p>
          ADGM hosts a large number of companies, holding structures, funds and family offices, so it is common for one spouse to own shares in an ADGM entity or to work for an ADGM employer. None of that moves the divorce into the ADGM Courts. The family court deciding your divorce, usually the Civil Family Court for non-Muslims in Abu Dhabi, deals with ADGM assets as part of the financial claims, just as it would with a mainland company or a bank account.
        </p>
        <h3>What that means in practice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> ADGM shareholdings, dividends, salary, bonuses and employment benefits belong in the financial picture that both spouses put before the family court. ADGM company registry records can help confirm who owns what.</li>
          <li><strong>Protecting assets:</strong> where there is a real risk that assets will be moved, the family court can order a precautionary attachment through the normal onshore procedures. There is no separate ADGM family remedy to apply for.</li>
          <li><strong>Valuation:</strong> if a spouse owns a business through an ADGM company, its value is assessed within the family case, usually with the help of an expert report.</li>
          <li><strong>Separate commercial disputes:</strong> a genuine dispute between shareholders, or with the company itself, can still go to the ADGM Courts on its own facts. That case runs alongside the divorce, not instead of it, and it does not decide the family claims.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If a business is at the centre of your finances, our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-business-owner-uae/">divorce when you own a business in the UAE</a> explains how shares, valuations and contribution claims work, and our <a href="/en/property-division-divorce-uae/">property division guide</a> covers real estate and joint assets. Assets held outside the UAE raise their own questions, which our guide to <a href="/en/offshore-assets-divorce-uae/">offshore assets in a UAE divorce</a> covers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>ADGM Wills and Estate Planning After Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          This is where ADGM genuinely matters to a divorcing couple. ADGM runs a wills registry for non-Muslims, and a registered ADGM will lets the assets it covers pass under your will rather than under default inheritance rules. The DIFC Wills Service Centre in Dubai offers a similar service, with probate in the DIFC Courts. Both are estate-planning tools. Neither has anything to do with how or where you divorce.
        </p>
        <p>
          A divorce is still a good moment to look at your will again. A will written during the marriage often names your spouse as the main beneficiary, as an executor, or as the person who would look after your children if something happened to you. Once the divorce and the financial arrangements are settled, those choices may no longer reflect what you want.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Review beneficiaries:</strong> check who receives your UAE property, accounts and business interests under your current will.</li>
          <li><strong>Check executors and guardians:</strong> decide whether your former spouse should keep any role, particularly as guardian of your children.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask how divorce affects your existing will:</strong> the effect of a divorce on an earlier will depends on the rules that govern it, so confirm the position with a lawyer rather than assuming the will updates itself. Many people make the update once the financial settlement is final.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The family lawyer handling your Abu Dhabi divorce can usually point you to a wills specialist. If you are still choosing representation, our guide to finding a <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-abu-dhabi/">divorce lawyer in Abu Dhabi</a> explains what to look for.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>DIFC, ADGM and the Civil Family Court Compared</h2>
        <p>
          The table sets the two financial-centre courts against the court that actually decides non-Muslim divorces in Abu Dhabi. The point is not that one is better than the others. They do different jobs.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Aspect</th>
                <th>DIFC Courts</th>
                <th>ADGM Courts</th>
                <th>Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {comparisonData.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <td class="td-highlight">{row.factor}</td>
                  <td>{row.difc}</td>
                  <td>{row.adgm}</td>
                  <td>{row.adcfc}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The rows that matter most are the divorce and family-claims rows. Only the Civil Family Court can end the marriage and decide custody, alimony and the financial claims, so asking the ADGM Courts for any of them wastes time and money. For the wider legal framework behind every UAE divorce, see our overview of <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <FAQSection items={faqItems} />

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Related Guides</h2>
